Here's how to handle each scenario with modern MSTest (3.9+/3.10+):

## 1. Windows-only tests — `[OSCondition]`

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[OSCondition(OperatingSystems.Windows)]
public void Registry_ReadsExpectedValue()
{
    var value = Registry.GetValue(@"HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\MyApp", "Setting", null);
    Assert.IsNotNull(value);
}
```

## 2. Skip in CI — `[CICondition]`

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[CICondition(ConditionMode.Exclude)]
public void RenderFrame_UsesLocalGpu()
{
    var result = GpuRenderer.Render(scene);
    Assert.IsNotNull(result);
}
```

## 3. Flaky external API — `[Retry]`

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[Retry(3)]
[Timeout(10000)]
public async Task GetWeather_Returnsforecast(TestContext testContext)
{
    var forecast = await _weatherClient.GetForecastAsync(testContext.CancellationToken);
    Assert.IsNotNull(forecast);
}
```

Use `[Retry]` only for genuinely flaky external dependencies—not to mask race conditions.

## 4. Cleanup even on failure — `[TestCleanup]` or `IDisposable`

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class TempFileTests : IDisposable
{
    private readonly string _tempFile;

    public TempFileTests()
    {
        _tempFile = Path.GetTempFileName();
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void WriteAndRead_RoundTrips()
    {
        File.WriteAllText(_tempFile, "hello");
        Assert.AreEqual("hello", File.ReadAllText(_tempFile));
    }

    // Runs even if the test fails
    public void Dispose()
    {
        if (File.Exists(_tempFile))
            File.Delete(_tempFile);
    }
}
```

Alternatively, use `[TestCleanup]` if you prefer the attribute style:

```csharp
[TestCleanup]
public void Cleanup()
{
    if (File.Exists(_tempFile))
        File.Delete(_tempFile);
}
```

Both `[TestCleanup]` and `Dispose` are guaranteed to run after each test regardless of pass/fail. Execution order is: `[TestCleanup]` → `DisposeAsync` → `Dispose`.

---

**Minimum versions:** `[Retry]` requires MSTest 3.9+, `[OSCondition]`/`[CICondition]` require MSTest 3.10+.
